#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
awsvpn-cli: headless AWS Client VPN client for SAML-federated (Entra ID)
endpoints. Python 3 stdlib only, drives openvpn3. No sudo, no GUI.

Why this exists
---------------
SAML sign-in on AWS Client VPN is an OpenVPN dynamic challenge that only the
AWS VPN Client implements, and only on Windows, macOS and Ubuntu x86_64.
Nothing exists for Android, and on Linux the GUI is the only way in. This tool
does the sign-in itself and hands the result to a stock OpenVPN 3 client.

How the sign-in works (two connections to the same gateway IP)
--------------------------------------------------------------
1. Connect with username "N/A", password "ACS::35001". The gateway refuses
   with a challenge:  AUTH_FAILED,CRV1:R:<sid>:<user>:<SAML sign-in URL>
2. Open the URL. Entra posts the SAML response to http://127.0.0.1:35001,
   where this tool listens.
3. Connect again with password  CRV1::<sid>::<SAMLResponse>  and the gateway
   lets you in. The sid is bound to the gateway node that issued it, so both
   connections go to one IP, resolved once.

Leg 1 is done here in Python: a minimal OpenVPN control channel (UDP or TCP,
TLS through ssl.MemoryBIO), which stops as soon as the challenge arrives. No
tunnel, no root, no openvpn binary. Leg 2 is a real tunnel:

    Linux    openvpn3 (openvpn3-linux). Stock openvpn 2.x cannot carry the
             leg-2 password (~10 kB; its control-channel buffer is 2048 bytes).
    Android  OpenVPN for Android (ics-openvpn, Google Play) with its OpenVPN 3
             core. This tool runs in Termux, signs in, and opens a profile with
             the password inline in the app: import, then connect.

             The password must never pass through one of the app's text
             fields. Android limits a single-line EditText without an explicit
             maxLength to 5000 characters, and both the connect dialog and the
             profile editor are such fields: they silently cut the ~10 kB
             password. Inline in an imported profile, it reaches the core whole.

The leg-2 password is single use. openvpn3 replays it on a reconnect and the
gateway refuses it, so a dropped tunnel needs a fresh `up`.

Usage
-----
    awsvpn-cli check    --profile ~/vpn/dev.ovpn    # leg 1 only, no sign-in
    awsvpn-cli up       --profile ~/vpn/dev.ovpn
    awsvpn-cli status   --profile ~/vpn/dev.ovpn
    awsvpn-cli down     --profile ~/vpn/dev.ovpn

    # Android, in the Termux app; tunnel run by OpenVPN for Android
    awsvpn-cli up       --profile ~/vpn/dev.ovpn    # sign in, import, connect

The profile is the .ovpn that `aws ec2 export-client-vpn-client-configuration`
or the Client VPN console gives you. Nothing organisation-specific is embedded.
"""

